# tests/tsan.supp
#
# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the cvm test suite.
#
# One pattern, and the reasoning matters more than the pattern.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reclaimed memory is reused
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A tracing collector hands memory from one thread to another. The previous
# owner performed no synchronisation to give it up — nothing gave it up; the
# collector *decided* the object was unreachable. So there is no
# happens-before edge from that thread's last write into the object to the
# next owner's first write, and ThreadSanitizer reports the pair as a race.
#
# The report is correct by its own definition and not a defect: the memory is
# unreachable, nobody reads the old value, and the next owner is entitled to
# treat it as fresh. This is a mismatch between the tool's model (every
# access to an address needs an edge) and a collector's (an unreachable
# object's storage is free).
#
# Every tracing collector has this, which is why bdwgc ships a suppression
# file of its own. The alternatives were both worse:
#
#   - annotate the allocator with __tsan_acquire/__tsan_release around every
#     hand-off. That tells the tool "this thread acquired the region", but
#     there is nothing on the other side to acquire from, so it does not
#     silence the report; it only adds calls to the allocation path.
#
#   - turn ThreadSanitizer off for the stress binaries. That would blind the
#     tool to real races in those tests, which are exactly the tests whose
#     whole purpose is to produce the interleavings that expose them.
#
# So the suppression is scoped to the functions that churn allocations, in
# the one binary where several threads allocate at once. A race inside the
# collector still reports, because its frames are in vm/ and rt/ rather than
# in a test worker — and that claim is checked rather than asserted: see the
# negative control note in TEST.md, which injects a deliberate race into the
# runtime and confirms it is still reported with this file in effect.
#
# The names are the thread entry points of tests/stress/test_stress_threads.c
# and tests/unit/test_trap.c, which are the only functions that allocate from
# more than one thread.
race:storm_worker
race:keeping_worker
race:allocate_in_a_loop
